<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · West Africa</p><h1 class="st-title">Wassa</h1><p class="st-meta">1650 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An Akan kingdom on the goldfields of south-western Ghana, long squeezed between Denkyira, the Ashanti Empire and European forts on the coast.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Wassa emerged in the 17th century in the forest between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pra-river">Pra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankobra-river">Ankobra</a> rivers, a region rich in alluvial gold around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarkwa">Tarkwa</a>. It was for a time subject to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/denkyira">Denkyira</a>, and after the collapse of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denkyira">Denkyira</a> in 1701 it passed under the influence of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/ashanti-empire">Ashanti Empire</a>. The state later divided into two branches, Wassa Fiase and Wassa Amenfi.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Each branch was ruled by a king chosen from a royal matrilineage and advised by chiefs and elders, in the common <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akan">Akan</a> pattern. Gold mining and trading supplied the coastal forts of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-gold-coast">Dutch Gold Coast</a> and the British, and control of the paths to the sea gave Wassa some leverage. It repeatedly allied with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fante">Fante</a> and the British against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> claims to the coast.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the British victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ashanti-empire">Ashanti</a> in 1874, Wassa was absorbed into the new <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/gold-coast-colony">Gold Coast Colony</a>. Its goldfields became the centre of the colonial mining industry from the 1870s, and the Wassa traditional areas survive within modern Ghana.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/wassa.jpg" alt="Wassa" width="320" height="384"><figcaption>Image: Wikimedia Commons · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:GRAMBERG(1861)_p007_ENNIMIR,_KONING_VAN_OOST-WASSA.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1650 CE – 1874 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Akan traditional religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Wassa (Akan)</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/ghana">Ghana</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kingdom of Wassa, Wassaw</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=wassa" aria-label="Wassa on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4666 1895 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Wassa lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M4949 2199h0M4995 2226h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4986 2151h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M4986 2151h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day Ghana. <a href="/atlas?year=1762&amp;focus=wassa">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

